      en: "The Barka is one of Eritrea's longest and most significant rivers, spanning over 640 kilometers. Originating in the central Eritrean highlands, it flows northwest across the country's vast western plains. Along its course, it passes the town of Agordat before crossing the Sudanese border, eventually ending in the Tokar Delta on the Red Sea. The Barka is a seasonal river, carrying water primarily during the summer rainy season and often turning into a sandy bed during the dry period. Despite its intermittency, it provides a vital water source for nomadic communities and agriculture in the Gash-Barka region. The Barka basin is also an important habitat for various wildlife and plays a central role in the ecological balance of Western Eritrea. Geography K7 — seasonal rivers and desert fringes."
